# Setup Instructions

## Prerequisites

- Node.js 20+ and npm
- A free [WeatherStack](https://weatherstack.com/) API key (used for current weather)
- A free [Visual Crossing](https://www.visualcrossing.com/weather-api) API key (used for historical/forecast weather)

## 1. Install dependencies

```
cd whats-the-weather-app
npm install
```

## 2. Configure environment variables

Copy the example file and fill in your own keys:

```
cp .env.example .env
```

`.env` needs four values:

```
VITE_WEATHERSTACK_API_KEY=your-weatherstack-key
VITE_WEATHERSTACK_API_BASEURL=https://api.weatherstack.com
VITE_VISUALCROSSING_API_KEY=your-visual-crossing-key
VITE_VISUALCROSSING_API_BASE_URL=https://weather.visualcrossing.com/VisualCrossingWebServices/rest/services/timeline
```

These are read client-side (`import.meta.env`), which is why they're prefixed with `VITE_`. There's no
backend in this project, so the keys are bundled into the client — see the Design Decisions notes for
the trade-off this implies.

## 3. Run the app locally

```
npm run dev
```

Vite will print a local URL.

## 4. Other useful scripts

- `npm run build` — type-checks (`tsc -b`) then produces a production build in `dist/`
- `npm run preview` — serves the production build locally to sanity-check it
- `npm test` — runs the Vitest suite
- `npm run lint` — runs ESLint

## Deployment

The app is deployed to Vercel. Because the Vite project lives in the `whats-the-weather-app`
subdirectory rather than the repo root, a `vercel.json` at the repo root points Vercel at the correct
install/build commands and output directory — no dashboard configuration is required beyond setting
the four environment variables above in the Vercel project settings.
